    "date_str": "2026-05-01",
    "description": "Joint Opposition Alliance seeks probe into diesel procurement deal, alleges irregularities and losses",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/opinion/Joint-Opposition-Alliance-seeks-probe-into-diesel-procurement-deal-alleges-irregularities-and-losses/14-791338",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1777593631.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-01-dailyftlk-5face85e-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d8e4eb78fe8a9e4846fb978c07c03e9e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1981,
    "chunk_text": "Joint Opposition Alliance seeks probe into diesel procurement deal, alleges irregularities and losses\n\nThe Joint Opposition Alliance members speak to the media after formally lodging a complaint with CIABOC\n\nThe Joint Opposition Alliance, a coalition representing several key political parties has formally lodged a complaint with the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption (CIABOC), calling for an investigation into an alleged irregular diesel procurement process involving the Ceylon Petroleum Corporation (CPC).\n\nFraming its submission as a matter of \u201csocial responsibility,\u201d the Alliance states that it is acting on information gathered from the public, organisations, and media reports. The complaint asserts that the alleged transaction may have resulted in significant financial losses to the State and calls for a comprehensive and impartial inquiry.\n\nThe complaint situates the procurement decision within the broader geopolitical context of the Gulf War February 2026, noting that US warships had been deployed in the Gulf region as early as January 26, 2026. According to the Joint Opposition Alliance, this development provided sufficient warning for Sri Lanka to build up fuel reserves in anticipation of supply disruptions.\n\nThe Joint Opposition Alliance further claims that national storage infrastructure, including facilities in Muthurajawela, Sapugaskanda, Trincomalee, and regional depots had the capacity to hold fuel stocks adequate for nearly two months of normal consumption. It also cites public statements made in early March by the CPC Chairman indicating that existing fuel reserves would last approximately 33 days.\n\nDespite these assurances, fuel queues reportedly began forming on February 28, raising questions about stock management and distribution. The Joint Opposition Alliance argues that such shortages cannot be attributed to hoarding by consumers, given the practical limitations on storing large fuel quantities."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-01-dailyftlk-5face85e",
    "num": "dailyftlk-5face85e",
    "date_str": "2026-05-01",
    "description": "Joint Opposition Alliance seeks probe into diesel procurement deal, alleges irregularities and losses",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/opinion/Joint-Opposition-Alliance-seeks-probe-into-diesel-procurement-deal-alleges-irregularities-and-losses/14-791338",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
