    "chunk_size_bytes": 1535,
    "chunk_text": "We have suffered a collective loss of consciousness. We all know what a chair is. We know what a perfectly designed bar counter looks like. We are a society fluent in the language of interior design, obsessing over the sterile, curated arrangements of our indoor sanctuaries. But almost none of us know the plants that make up a garden.\n\nAnd while everyone is busy matching their yoga mats to the decor, the infrastructure groans, buckles, and breaks.\n\nThe cruelest joke of Balification is the silent, relentless financial extraction that accompanies it. Every overpriced flat white and every night spent in a sterile concrete villa\u2014complete with a bite-sized swimming pool and a garden choked with the same hegemonous ornamentals\u2014is heavily taxed. The government skims an 18% Value Added Tax, a 1% Tourism Development Levy, and a 2.5% Social Security Contribution Levy. The state is gorging itself on this hollow aesthetic boom.\n\nIt certainly does not return to the soil. It does not fund anything as foundational as waste collection. Instead, the burden of this imported, unchecked consumption falls directly onto the earth, transforming the island into a bi-coastal wasteland. In the East, staggering 5x5km trash dumps fester kilometers inland, suffocating the primary dry forests of ancient palu trees. In the South, toxic runoff bleeds directly into the ocean at Habaraduwa. The island is drowning while the treasury swells on its peril.\n\nIf the state is collecting millions, how can it fail to secure a single train crossing?"
  },
